-- New-style daily words for 21 Nov – 31 Dec (days 325–365): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"),
-- a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before. Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style,
-- so anything already rewritten in Admin → Devotions is kept. The words repeat every year.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'After the flood, Noah stepped out onto a washed-out world. Everything familiar was gone. And the first thing God gave him was not a new house or a harvest, but a promise: as long as the earth remains, seedtime and harvest will not stop.

That promise is still holding. Every rainy season, every planting, every harvest in Nigeria and everywhere else is God keeping His word. The farmer who plants maize in April is trusting that promise, whether he knows it or not.

The same law works in your life. What you plant in prayer, in kindness, in honest work and in the Word does not disappear into the ground. It is waiting for its season. Some seeds take a week, some take years, but God has not forgotten a single one.

Today: think of one good seed you have been sowing for a long time without seeing results. Thank God for the harvest that is coming, before you see it.',
  declaration = 'I am sowing good seed, and my harvest is sure. God keeps His promises, season after season.',
  prayer = 'Faithful God, thank You that You keep Your word in every season. Where I have sown in tears and seen nothing yet, give me patience to keep sowing. Guard every good seed in my life until it becomes a harvest that brings You glory. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy325' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul spends two whole chapters of 2 Corinthians talking about money, giving and generosity. Then he stops, and you can almost hear him sigh with joy: "Thanks be unto God for his unspeakable gift."

He has suddenly remembered the gift behind every gift. We give because God first gave, and what He gave cannot be measured or properly described. Jesus is the gift that words cannot hold.

It is easy to thank God for the things we can see: a job, a house, a child, a healed body. Those are good, and we should give thanks for them. But even if every one of them were taken away, we would still have the greatest gift. We would still have Him.

This is what makes a believer''s gratitude different. It does not rise and fall with our circumstances, because its deepest reason never changes.

Today: when you pray, spend the first minute thanking God for Jesus Himself, before you mention anything you need.',
  declaration = 'Jesus is God''s unspeakable gift to me. Whatever I have or lack, I have Him, and I am grateful.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for Jesus, the gift I could never earn or repay. Forgive me for the times I have valued Your gifts more than You. Let my heart overflow with thanks today, not only for what You give, but for who You are. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy326' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In ancient Israel, land was shared out by measuring lines. Each family received a portion, and that portion was their inheritance. David looks at his own life and says the lines have fallen for him "in pleasant places".

David''s life was not easy. He was hunted by a king, betrayed by friends, and grieved over his own children. Yet when he looked at what God had given him, he called it a goodly heritage.

Comparison steals this joy. We measure our portion against someone else''s and feel short-changed. Social media makes it worse, showing us everybody''s best moments while we live our ordinary ones.

But your lines were drawn by a loving Father. Your family, your gifts, your city, even your limitations are part of a portion He chose with care. There is more good in your life than you notice when you are busy comparing.

Today: write down five good things inside the boundaries of your own life, and thank God for each one by name.',
  declaration = 'The lines have fallen to me in pleasant places. I will not despise my portion; I have a goodly heritage in God.',
  prayer = 'Lord, forgive me for comparing my life to others and missing the good You have placed around me. Open my eyes to see my heritage as You see it. Teach me contentment and gratitude in my own portion. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy327' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'A sacrifice costs something. In the Old Testament it might be a lamb or a bull. Hebrews says that, through Jesus, we now offer a different sacrifice: the praise of our lips, offered continually.

Why would praise be a sacrifice? Because sometimes it costs us our feelings. Praising God when the results are good is easy. Praising Him when the doctor''s report is unclear, when the rent is due, when a prayer seems unanswered, takes faith.

That is exactly when praise is most precious. It says, "I do not understand everything, but I know who You are." Paul and Silas sang in prison with their backs bleeding, and the doors opened. Not every prison opens that quickly, but every heart that praises in the dark is changed by it.

Praise is not a mood you wait for. It is a choice you make, again and again, all through the day.

Today: at three different times today, morning, afternoon and night, stop and say one sentence of praise to God out loud, whatever you are feeling.',
  declaration = 'I will offer the sacrifice of praise continually. My circumstances will not silence my thanksgiving.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, through You I bring my praise to God. When I do not feel like praising, help me to choose it anyway. Let my lips give thanks to Your name in every situation today, and let my praise rise above my fears. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy328' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"His greatness is unsearchable." You could study God for a thousand years and still be at the beginning. There is no bottom to His wisdom, no edge to His power, no end to His goodness.

Then the psalm says something practical: "One generation shall praise thy works to another." The greatness of God is meant to be passed on. Parents tell children. Grandparents tell grandchildren. Older believers tell new ones.

Many of us came to faith because someone told us what God had done. A mother who prayed through hard years. A pastor who never gave up on a community. A friend who shared her testimony at the right moment.

Now it is your turn. Somebody younger is watching how you speak about God. Your stories of His faithfulness may be the very thing that keeps them going one day.

Today: tell one person younger than you, a child, a sibling, a colleague, about something God has done for you.',
  declaration = 'Great is the Lord and greatly to be praised. I will declare His mighty acts to the next generation.',
  prayer = 'Great God, Your greatness is beyond my understanding, and I worship You. Give me courage to tell others what You have done. Let the young people around me grow up hearing about Your faithfulness from my lips. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy329' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Joel prophesied after a terrible season. Locusts had stripped the land bare. Crops, vines and fig trees were gone. The people had little to eat and little reason to hope.

Into that devastation God spoke: "I will restore to you the years that the locust hath eaten." And then this promise: you will eat in plenty, be satisfied, and praise the name of the Lord who dealt wondrously with you. "My people shall never be ashamed."

Maybe you know what a locust season feels like. Lost years. Lost money. Lost relationships or opportunities. It can feel as if something has eaten away a part of your life that you can never get back.

God specialises in restoration. He does not always give back the exact thing you lost, but He can fill the empty places so completely that you end up praising Him for what He has done. Shame does not get the final word over God''s people.

Today: bring one "locust-eaten" area of your life to God in prayer, and ask Him plainly to restore it.',
  declaration = 'God is restoring the years the locust has eaten. I will eat in plenty, be satisfied, and I will not be ashamed.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You see the losses I carry. I bring You the years and the things that have been eaten away. Restore what only You can restore, and let my life become a testimony of how wondrously You deal with Your people. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy330' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 67 begins like the priestly blessing: "God be merciful unto us, and bless us." But it does not stop at "us". The whole psalm looks outward. The reason for the blessing is that God''s way may be known upon earth, and His saving health among all nations.

So when it says "the earth shall yield her increase" and "God shall bless us", the next line follows: "and all the ends of the earth shall fear him." Our blessing has a purpose beyond our own comfort.

This changes how we ask God for things. It is right to pray for increase in our finances, our health, our families and our work. But we pray with open hands, ready for that increase to flow through us to others.

A blessed believer is a signpost. When people see God''s goodness in your life, and see you sharing it, they begin to ask about the God behind it.

Today: use something God has given you, money, time, a skill, a meal, to bless one person who cannot repay you.',
  declaration = 'God blesses me so that I can be a blessing. Through my life, people will come to know Him.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for every increase in my life. Let none of it stop with me. Make me a channel of Your goodness, so that the people around me see You and come to honour You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy331' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The disciples had just come back from buying food in a Samaritan town. Jesus had just had a life-changing conversation with a Samaritan woman at a well, and now she was running through the town telling everyone about Him.

The disciples saw fields that needed four more months to ripen. Jesus saw people coming out of the town towards Him and said, "Lift up your eyes. The fields are white already to harvest."

It is easy to think that sharing our faith is for later: when we know more, when the timing is perfect, when people seem ready. Jesus says the harvest is now. People around you are more open than you think: the colleague going through a divorce, the neighbour whose child is sick, the friend asking deep questions late at night.

You do not need to preach a sermon. Often the harvest begins with noticing someone, praying for them, and being willing to say what Jesus means to you.

Today: lift up your eyes. Ask God to show you one person who is ready for a kind word, a prayer, or an invitation.',
  declaration = 'I will lift up my eyes. The harvest is ready, and I am willing to be used by God today.',
  prayer = 'Lord of the harvest, open my eyes to the people around me who are ready to know You. Take away my fear and excuses. Give me the right words at the right time, and use my life to draw someone closer to You today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy332' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David tried to count God''s goodness and gave up. "If I would declare and speak of them, they are more than can be numbered." It is the happiest kind of failure there is.

Notice that he mentions two things: God''s wonderful works, and His thoughts toward us. God does not only act on our behalf; He thinks about us. You are on His mind. Another psalm says His thoughts toward you outnumber the sand (Psalm 139:17-18).

We usually find it easier to count our problems. They are loud and they keep reminding us they are there. Blessings are often quiet. They become part of ordinary life, and we stop seeing them: breath in our lungs, food on the table, people who love us, the grace that kept us through the last difficult season.

Counting God''s goodness is a habit that has to be practised. The more you practise it, the more you see.

Today: try to list twenty things God has done for you this year. If you reach twenty easily, keep going and see how far you get.',
  declaration = 'God''s wonderful works toward me are more than I can number. His thoughts toward me are good.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that I am always on Your mind. Train my eyes to see Your goodness in the ordinary moments of my life. Let gratitude grow in me until it drowns out every complaint. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy333' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 30 was written for the dedication of a house, but it reads like the testimony of someone who has come through a very dark season. "Weeping may endure for a night, but joy cometh in the morning." Then this verse: "Thou hast turned for me my mourning into dancing."

Sackcloth was rough cloth people wore when they were grieving. David says God took it off him and dressed him in gladness instead. Only God can change clothes like that, from the inside out.

And David tells us why: "to the end that my glory may sing praise to thee, and not be silent." God''s deliverance is meant to become praise. Our testimonies are not just for our own relief; they are for His glory.

As this month of thanksgiving comes to an end, look back at your year. Where were you wearing sackcloth? What has God turned around? Even if some sorrows are still with you, there are places where He has already brought you into dancing.

Today: write down one sorrow God turned into joy this year, and thank Him for it out loud.',
  declaration = 'God has turned my mourning into dancing. I will not be silent; I will give Him thanks forever.',
  prayer = 'Lord my God, thank You for every night of weeping that ended in a morning of joy. Where I still mourn, I trust You to bring me into gladness. I will not keep silent about Your goodness. I will give thanks to You forever. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy334' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 26 is a song for the day when God''s people are safe in a strong city. Its key thought comes a few verses earlier: "Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee." Then this prayer: "LORD, thou wilt ordain peace for us."

To ordain something is to decree it, to set it in place. Peace is not luck. It is not simply the absence of trouble. It is something God Himself establishes for His people, often right in the middle of trouble.

The second half of the verse is just as freeing: "thou also hast wrought all our works in us." Every good thing in you, every prayer you have prayed, every right choice you have made, God was working it in you. You are not carrying your spiritual life alone.

As December begins, many people feel pressure: end-of-year bills, travel, family expectations, unfinished goals. God offers a better way to finish the year, with a mind stayed on Him and a heart at rest.

Today: when you feel pressure rising, stop, breathe slowly, and say: "Lord, You have ordained peace for me."',
  declaration = 'God has ordained peace for me. Every good work in my life is His work, and my mind is stayed on Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, as this last month of the year begins, I receive the peace You have ordained for me. Keep my mind stayed on You when pressure rises. Thank You for every good work You have done in me this year. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy335' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus spoke these words on the night before the cross. He knew Judas was about to betray Him, Peter was about to deny Him, and the disciples were about to scatter. Yet what He gave them that night was peace: "my peace I give unto you."

His peace is different from the world''s. The world gives peace when things go well: when the money comes in, when the test results are good, when everybody is happy with you. Take those things away and that peace leaves with them.

Jesus'' peace was His own peace, the peace He carried into Gethsemane and before Pilate. It does not depend on outside conditions, because it comes from His presence with us.

Then He gives a command: "Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid." He would not command something impossible. With His peace inside you, you can refuse to let fear take over your heart.

Today: name the thing that troubles your heart most right now. Then say, "Jesus, I receive Your peace over this," and refuse to rehearse the fear.',
  declaration = 'I have the peace of Jesus, not the peace of the world. I will not let my heart be troubled or afraid.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for giving me Your own peace. I bring You the things that trouble my heart. I choose to receive Your peace instead of fear. Guard my heart and mind today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy336' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah was looking at a broken nation and praying for God to come down and act. In the middle of that prayer he says something astonishing: since the world began, no ear has heard and no eye has seen any God like You, who works for the person who waits for Him.

Other gods in Isaiah''s day had to be carried and served. The true God acts on behalf of those who wait for Him. Paul later quotes this verse in 1 Corinthians 2:9: "Eye hath not seen, nor ear heard... the things which God hath prepared for them that love him."

Waiting on God is not wasted time. It is not sitting idle. It is a posture of trust: looking to Him, obeying what you already know, and refusing to make your own way out of panic.

Some of what God has prepared for you, you cannot see yet. If you could see it, it would not take faith to wait for it. But He is preparing, even now.

Today: think of something you have been waiting on God for. Tell Him plainly, "I will wait for You," and ask for grace to keep trusting.',
  declaration = 'My God works for those who wait for Him. What He has prepared for me is greater than I can imagine.',
  prayer = 'Father, there is no God like You. Thank You that You act on behalf of those who wait for You. Help me not to rush ahead of You or give up. I trust that You are preparing good things for me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy337' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'We usually think of ourselves as the ones waiting on God. This verse turns it around: "therefore will the LORD wait, that he may be gracious unto you."

Isaiah was speaking to people who had refused God''s way and run to Egypt for help instead. God had every right to give up on them. Instead He waited, longing for the moment when they would turn back so that He could show them mercy.

That is the heart of our Father. He is not waiting to punish you. He is waiting to be gracious. Like the father in Jesus'' story of the prodigal son, He is watching the road for your return.

The verse ends with a blessing: "blessed are all they that wait for him." When we wait for Him and He waits to be gracious to us, we meet in the place of His mercy.

If you have wandered this year, in big ways or small, today is a good day to come home. You will not find a closed door.

Today: be honest with God about one area where you have run your own way. Turn back to Him, and receive His grace.',
  declaration = 'The Lord is waiting to be gracious to me. I turn to Him, and I receive His mercy today.',
  prayer = 'Gracious Father, thank You for waiting for me with mercy, not anger. I turn from the ways I have run without You. Receive me again, and let me walk in Your grace. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy338' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Bethlehem was a small town, "little among the thousands of Judah". It had no palace and no great army. Yet Micah prophesied, about seven hundred years before Jesus was born, that the eternal Ruler of Israel would come out of that little place.

God loves to work through small things. A shepherd boy from Bethlehem became King David. A baby born in a Bethlehem stable became the Saviour of the world.

Maybe you feel small. Your town is not famous. Your family has no connections. Your ministry, business or gift seems too little to matter. But God is not limited by size. What He chooses, He makes great.

The verse also says this Ruler''s "goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting." The child of Bethlehem existed before Bethlehem did. Christmas is not the beginning of Jesus; it is God stepping into our small world.

Today: thank God for one "small" thing in your life that He could use greatly, and offer it to Him again.',
  declaration = 'God does great things through small places and small people. My life is not too little for His purpose.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You chose little Bethlehem for the birth of the King. I offer You my small things, my gifts, my town, my efforts. Use them for Your glory, and let Your greatness show through my weakness. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy339' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'King Ahaz was terrified. Two kings had joined forces against Jerusalem, and his heart shook "as the trees of the wood are moved with the wind." God offered him a sign. Ahaz refused to ask. So God gave a sign anyway: "a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."

Immanuel means "God with us." Matthew tells us that the birth of Jesus fulfilled this promise. God did not send advice from a distance. He came Himself.

This is the heart of Christmas, and the heart of the Christian life. You are not facing your fears alone. Whatever armies line up against you, whatever news shakes your heart like trees in the wind, God is with you.

His presence does not always remove the battle immediately. But it changes everything about how you face it. With God with you, you can stand.

Today: wherever you go today, remind yourself at least three times: "God is with me, here."',
  declaration = 'Jesus is Immanuel, God with us. God is with me today, and I will not be afraid.',
  prayer = 'Immanuel, thank You that You did not stay far away, but came to be with us. When my heart is shaken, remind me that You are with me. Let Your presence be my peace and my strength today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy340' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In the ancient world, night watchmen stood on the city walls through the dark hours. They could not make the sun rise. They could only stay awake and keep watching. But they knew one thing for certain: morning would come.

The psalmist says his soul waits for the Lord "more than they that watch for the morning", and he repeats it, as if to say: this is how much I long for Him.

Psalm 130 begins "Out of the depths have I cried unto thee." The writer is in a dark place. But he waits with hope, because his hope is anchored in God''s word: "in his word do I hope."

Your night may feel long. The answer you are waiting for may not have come. But God''s word is as certain as the sunrise. The watchman does not doubt the morning just because it is still dark.

Today: choose one promise from God''s word that speaks to your situation. Write it down and read it whenever the night feels long.',
  declaration = 'My soul waits for the Lord, and in His word I hope. As surely as morning comes, God will come through for me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I wait for You more than the watchman waits for the morning. When the night feels long, anchor my hope in Your word. I believe my morning is coming. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy341' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Micah lived in a painful time. He describes a society where the good man had perished, friends could not be trusted, and even family members turned against each other. Then he makes a decision: "Therefore I will look unto the LORD."

When people disappoint us, we are tempted to become bitter, or to stop trusting anyone at all. Micah takes a third way. He turns his eyes from people to God.

He says three things. I will look to the Lord. I will wait for the God of my salvation. My God will hear me. Notice the confidence in the last line. Not "my God might hear me." My God will hear me.

Maybe someone has let you down this year: a friend, a spouse, a church member, a boss. The hurt is real. But God is not like the people who failed you. He will hear you.

Today: bring one disappointment to God in prayer. Release the person to Him, and look to the Lord for what you need.',
  declaration = 'I will look to the Lord. I will wait for the God of my salvation. My God will hear me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You know the people who have disappointed me. I release them to You, and I refuse bitterness. My eyes are on You. Thank You that You hear me, and that You will never fail me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy342' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Kings usually arrive on war horses with soldiers around them. Zechariah prophesied a different King: just, bringing salvation, and "lowly, and riding upon an ass."

About five hundred years later, Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a young donkey, and the crowds spread their clothes on the road and shouted "Hosanna." Matthew and John both point back to this verse.

Our King does not come to crush us. He comes humbly, with salvation in His hands. His power is real, but it is gentle power, the power of a King who would rather serve than be served.

This is good news for anyone who feels unworthy. You do not have to impress Him or be afraid of Him. He comes to you, lowly and kind.

It is also a pattern for us. If our King chose humility, we can too: in how we treat people who serve us, in how we use our authority, in how we handle success.

Today: welcome Jesus as King over one area of your life you have been keeping for yourself. Then choose one humble act of service for someone else.',
  declaration = 'My King comes to me just, humble and bringing salvation. I welcome Him and I follow His humble way.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, my lowly and righteous King, I rejoice that You come to me with salvation. Rule over every part of my life. Teach me to walk in humility as You did. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy343' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 9 describes a coming King whose government and peace will keep on increasing without end. It is not a kingdom that rises and then falls, like every human government in history. It grows forever.

Then the verse ends with a guarantee: "The zeal of the LORD of hosts will perform this." It does not depend on politicians, elections or human strength. God''s own passion will make it happen.

When we look at the news, it is easy to feel hopeless: corruption, insecurity, rising prices, conflict. These things are real, and we should pray and work for justice. But they are not the end of the story. Jesus'' kingdom is increasing, quietly and steadily, in hearts and homes and communities across the world.

You are part of that increase. Every time you choose justice over shortcuts, peace over revenge, and truth over convenience, His government grows in one more place.

Today: pray for your country and your leaders by name. Then do one just and peaceful thing in your own corner.',
  declaration = 'The government and peace of Jesus keep increasing in the earth. The zeal of the Lord will perform it, and I am part of it.',
  prayer = 'Lord of hosts, thank You that the kingdom of Jesus has no end. I pray for my nation and its leaders: let justice and peace increase. Let Your kingdom grow in my own life, my home and my work. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy344' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The angel Gabriel appeared to a young woman in Nazareth, a small town people looked down on. His first words were "Hail, thou that art highly favoured." Mary was troubled. So before he explained God''s plan, Gabriel said, "Fear not, Mary: for thou hast found favour with God."

God''s favour often arrives before we understand His plan. Mary did not yet know about the stable, the shepherds, or the long road ahead. She only knew that God was with her and pleased to use her.

Favour does not mean an easy life. Mary''s yes would cost her: misunderstanding, a difficult journey, and one day, standing at the foot of the cross. But favour means God has chosen to work through you, and He will carry you through what that requires.

Many of us hear God''s call and immediately feel afraid: I am not ready, I am not qualified, what will people say? God''s answer is the same as Gabriel''s: fear not. You have found favour.

Today: if God has been stirring something in you, a step of obedience, a new assignment, tell Him: "I receive Your favour, and I will not be afraid."',
  declaration = 'I have found favour with God. I will not fear what He calls me to do, because He is with me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for Your favour on my life. When Your plans are bigger than my understanding, help me not to be afraid. Like Mary, let me trust You before I see the full picture. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy345' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Mary asked a fair question: "How shall this be, seeing I know not a man?" Gabriel explained that the Holy Spirit would come upon her. Then he told her about her relative Elizabeth, old and once called barren, now six months pregnant. And he ended with this: "For with God nothing shall be impossible."

Mary''s response is one of the most beautiful sentences in the Bible: "Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it unto me according to thy word."

Faith is not pretending we have no questions. Mary asked hers. Faith is saying yes to God after we have asked, even when the answer is bigger than we can understand.

Some of the things you are praying about look impossible: a healed body, a restored marriage, a breakthrough at work, a family member coming to Christ. The impossible is God''s normal territory.

Today: take your "impossible" situation and pray Mary''s prayer over it: "Lord, be it unto me according to Your word."',
  declaration = 'With God nothing shall be impossible. Be it unto me according to His word.',
  prayer = 'Lord, nothing is impossible for You. I bring You the situations that look impossible to me. Like Mary, I say yes to Your word and Your will. Be it unto me according to Your word. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy346' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'When Mary visited Elizabeth, the baby in Elizabeth''s womb leapt for joy, and Mary burst into a song we call the Magnificat. "My soul doth magnify the Lord, and my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour."

Why was she rejoicing? "For he hath regarded the low estate of his handmaiden." God noticed her. Not because she was rich or famous or powerful, but because He looks with love on the lowly.

To magnify something is to make it look bigger. A magnifying glass does not change the size of what you look at, but it helps you see how great it really is. Praise does that with God. It does not make Him bigger, but it helps us see how great He is.

When we magnify our problems, they fill our whole view. When we magnify the Lord, everything else is seen in its proper size.

You may feel overlooked: at work, in your family, even in church. God has not overlooked you. He regards you.

Today: spend five minutes magnifying the Lord, telling Him who He is and what He has done, before you mention any of your needs.',
  declaration = 'My soul magnifies the Lord. He sees me in my low estate, and I rejoice in God my Saviour.',
  prayer = 'My Saviour, I magnify You. Thank You for seeing me when I feel small and overlooked. Let my praise make You great in my eyes, and let my problems shrink in Your light. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy347' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Malachi is the last book of the Old Testament. After it come about four hundred years when no new prophet spoke. Its final chapter holds this promise: "unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings."

Picture the sun rising after a long, cold night. Its warmth spreads across the land and things begin to grow again. That is the picture of Jesus'' coming: light after darkness, healing after sickness, life after a long silence.

The verse ends with a joyful image: "ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall." A calf let out of the stall after the winter jumps and runs with sheer joy. That is what freedom in Christ is meant to feel like.

Jesus brings healing in many forms: for bodies, for broken hearts, for damaged relationships, for shame we have carried for years. Bring whatever needs healing into the light of His presence.

Today: ask Jesus, the Sun of righteousness, to shine on one area of your life that needs healing, and thank Him in advance for His healing touch.',
  declaration = 'The Sun of righteousness has risen on me with healing in His wings. I go forth free and full of joy.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, Sun of righteousness, rise over my life. Shine on every place of sickness, sorrow and shame. Bring Your healing, and let me go forth free and rejoicing. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy348' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Zacharias had been unable to speak for nine months because he doubted the angel''s message. When his son John was born and named, his mouth was opened, and he prophesied. His song ends with these words about Jesus: "the dayspring from on high hath visited us."

The dayspring is the first light of dawn. Zacharias says this light comes "through the tender mercy of our God". Not because we earned it, but because God is tender-hearted toward people in darkness.

And look at who it is for: "them that sit in darkness and in the shadow of death." Not people walking confidently, but people sitting, stuck, unable to find their way. Jesus came to them, and He comes to us when we are stuck in the same way.

Then He does more than shine: He guides "our feet into the way of peace." He does not just show us where we are; He leads us where we need to go.

Today: if you feel stuck in some area of your life, ask Jesus to guide your feet into the way of peace, and take the next small step He shows you.',
  declaration = 'The dayspring from on high has visited me. Jesus shines in my darkness and guides my feet into the way of peace.',
  prayer = 'God of tender mercy, thank You for sending Jesus to people sitting in darkness. Where I feel stuck, shine Your light. Guide my feet into the way of peace, one step at a time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy349' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"Arise, shine; for thy light is come." Isaiah does not tell God''s people to produce their own light. He tells them to rise up because light has already come to them: "the glory of the LORD is risen upon thee."

Then he is honest about the world: "darkness shall cover the earth, and gross darkness the people." He does not pretend things are fine. But in the middle of that darkness, God''s glory is seen on His people.

This is our calling, especially in December. Many people around us are lonely, grieving, broke or afraid during the holidays. The darker it gets, the more clearly light shows.

You do not shine by trying to look impressive. You shine by reflecting Him: kindness when others are harsh, hope when others despair, honesty when others cut corners, generosity when others hoard.

Some of us have been sitting down spiritually, tired or discouraged. The word today is simple: arise. Get up. Your light has come.

Today: do one thing that brings light into someone''s darkness: a visit, a call, a meal, a gift, or a prayer with them.',
  declaration = 'I arise and shine, for my light has come. The glory of the Lord is risen upon me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I get up from discouragement and tiredness. Let Your glory rise on me and shine through me. Show me who needs Your light today, and use me to bring it. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy350' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"When the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth his Son." Jesus was not born a day too early or a day too late. God had been preparing history for centuries: the prophets, the promises, even the Roman roads that would carry the gospel across the world.

God''s timing often looks slow to us. Israel waited centuries for the Messiah. But when the time was full, God acted, and He did not miss a detail.

Why did Jesus come? "To redeem them that were under the law, that we might receive the adoption of sons." Redemption means buying back. Adoption means being brought into the family with full rights. Jesus did not only free us; He made us children of God.

If you are waiting on God''s timing in your own life, take comfort from Christmas. He knows the fulness of time for your situation too, and He is never late.

And remember who you are: not a servant hoping to be noticed, but an adopted son or daughter, loved and secure in your Father''s house.

Today: say aloud, "I am a child of God, adopted and loved," and thank God that His timing in your life is perfect.',
  declaration = 'God''s timing is perfect. Through Jesus I am redeemed and adopted as a child of God.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for sending Your Son at exactly the right time. Thank You that through Him I am redeemed and adopted into Your family. Help me trust Your timing in every part of my life. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy351' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In ancient times there were no phones or newspapers. News came by runners. Watchmen on the city walls would see a messenger coming over the mountains and wonder: is it good news or bad?

Isaiah describes the joy of seeing a messenger bringing good news: peace, salvation, and the words "Thy God reigneth!" Even his dusty, tired feet look beautiful, because of what he carries.

Paul quotes this verse in Romans 10 when he talks about preaching the gospel. Every believer carries good news. And this season is one of the most natural times of year to share it. People are thinking about Christmas, family, the year ending. Many are more open than usual.

Your feet become beautiful when they carry Jesus into places of bad news: a hospital room, a grieving home, a friend''s worried conversation.

Today: invite someone to a Christmas service or carol event, or share in a simple message what Jesus means to you this Christmas.',
  declaration = 'My feet carry good news. I bring peace and salvation, and I declare: our God reigns!',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the good news that saved me. Make my feet beautiful by sending me to people who need to hear it. Give me boldness and gentleness to share Jesus this Christmas. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy352' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God gave Aaron and his sons these words to speak over the people of Israel. It is one of the oldest blessings in the Bible, and God Himself wrote it. Then He added: "they shall put my name upon the children of Israel; and I will bless them."

Listen to what God wants for you. He wants to bless you and keep you. He wants His face to shine on you, the way a parent''s face lights up when a child walks in. He wants to be gracious to you, to look upon you with favour, and to give you peace.

This is not a reluctant God who must be persuaded to bless. This is His own heart, written down so that we would never forget it.

As we come close to Christmas, many families will gather. Words spoken in families can wound or bless. What if you were the one who spoke blessing?

Today: speak this blessing over someone by name: a child, a spouse, a parent, a friend. Then receive it for yourself, too.',
  declaration = 'The Lord blesses me and keeps me. His face shines on me, He is gracious to me, and He gives me peace.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that You want to bless me. Let Your face shine on me and my family. Teach me to speak blessing and not curses over the people I love. Give us Your peace this Christmas. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy353' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Malachi promised that God would send a messenger to prepare the way, and then "the Lord, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple." The New Testament tells us the messenger was John the Baptist, and the Lord who came was Jesus.

Notice the words "whom ye seek" and "whom ye delight in". The people had been longing for God to come. When He came, He came suddenly.

God''s moves often feel sudden after a long wait. Joseph waited years in prison, then was suddenly in the palace in a single day. The disciples waited in the upper room, then suddenly there was a sound from heaven at Pentecost.

But Malachi also asks: "who may abide the day of his coming?" When God comes, He purifies like a refiner''s fire. His presence is wonderful, but it also exposes what needs to change.

Preparing the way for the Lord, then, means making room: removing clutter, confessing sin, and setting our hearts on Him.

Today: ask God to show you one thing that needs to be cleared out of your heart to make room for Him this Christmas, and deal with it.',
  declaration = 'I prepare the way for the Lord in my heart. The Lord I seek is coming, and I make room for Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I seek You and I delight in You. Come and fill my heart this Christmas. Show me anything that needs to be cleared away, and purify me like a refiner. I make room for You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy354' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah saw a child being born and gave Him names that no ordinary child could carry: Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.

Each name meets a need. When life is dull and heavy, He is Wonderful. When you do not know what to do, He is your Counsellor. When you are weak, He is the mighty God. When you feel abandoned, He is the everlasting Father who never leaves. When everything inside you is in turmoil, He is the Prince of Peace.

And notice the words "unto us". Unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given. Jesus was not born for a few special people. He was born for us, for you.

"The government shall be upon his shoulder." Whatever weight you have carried this year, there is a shoulder strong enough to carry it. You were never meant to rule your life alone.

Today: choose the one name of Jesus you need most this week. Pray it to Him by name, for example: "Jesus, be my Counsellor in this decision."',
  declaration = 'Unto me a child is born. Jesus is my Wonderful Counsellor, my mighty God, my everlasting Father and my Prince of Peace.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You are Wonderful, Counsellor, the mighty God, the everlasting Father and the Prince of Peace. I place the weight of my life on Your shoulder. Be to me today exactly who I need You to be. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy355' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Joseph had a heartbreaking problem. Mary, the woman he was engaged to, was pregnant, and he knew he was not the father. He decided to end the engagement quietly. Then an angel spoke to him in a dream: do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife. And then this instruction: "thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins."

The name Jesus comes from the Hebrew name Joshua, which means "the Lord saves." His name is His mission. He did not come mainly to make us comfortable, successful or famous. He came to save us from our sins.

That is the best news of Christmas. Sin is what separates us from God and spoils everything it touches. Jesus came to deal with it at the root: forgiving the guilt, breaking the power, and one day removing it completely.

Joseph obeyed the angel, even though it cost him his reputation. Sometimes receiving Jesus means trusting God''s word over people''s opinions.

Today: if there is any sin you have been holding on to, bring it to Jesus. He came for exactly that. Receive His forgiveness and walk in it.',
  declaration = 'Jesus came to save me from my sins. I am forgiven, and sin has no power over me.',
  prayer = 'Jesus, Saviour, thank You for coming to save me from my sins. I confess my sins to You now, and I receive Your forgiveness. Break every power of sin over my life, and help me walk in freedom. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy356' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'It was census time, and Bethlehem was crowded. Mary was about to give birth, and "there was no room for them in the inn." So the Son of God was laid in a manger, a feeding trough for animals.

God could have chosen a palace. He chose a place where anyone could come: shepherds from the fields, poor and rich, without an appointment. From His first night on earth, Jesus was approachable.

The inn had no room, and many hearts still have no room. It is rarely because people hate Jesus. Usually they are simply full: busy, distracted, crowded with other things. December can be the busiest month of all, full of shopping, travel and parties, with Jesus left outside.

The good news is that Jesus is not too proud to come into a humble place. He does not wait for your life to be tidy or impressive. He comes into the stable of an ordinary heart that makes room for Him.

Today: before the Christmas rush takes over, set aside fifteen quiet minutes to sit with Jesus. Tell Him, "There is room for You here."',
  declaration = 'There is room for Jesus in my heart and in my home. I will not let busyness crowd Him out.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You came to a manger because there was no room in the inn. Forgive me for the times my life has been too crowded for You. I make room for You now. Come into every part of my life this Christmas. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy357' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'It was an ordinary night for the shepherds. They were doing their usual work, watching their flocks in the fields near Bethlehem. Then suddenly the sky was filled with angels praising God: "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men."

Heaven could not stay quiet. The birth of Jesus was the most important event in history, and the angels had to celebrate it.

Their song has two parts. Glory to God: everything that happened in Bethlehem was for His honour. And peace on earth: Jesus came to bring peace between God and people, and then between people and one another.

Christmas Eve is a good night to join their song. In the middle of preparations, cooking and travel, stop and give God the glory. And look for one place where you can bring peace on earth: a broken relationship to mend, a quarrel to end, a person to forgive.

Today: sing or say a Christmas song of praise to God tonight with your family or friends. Then make peace with one person you have been at odds with.',
  declaration = 'Glory to God in the highest! I join heaven''s song, and I bring His peace on earth wherever I go.',
  prayer = 'Glory to You, God in the highest! Thank You for sending Jesus to bring peace on earth. Fill my home with Your peace tonight. Help me to make peace where there has been strife. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy358' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Merry Christmas!

The shepherds were terrified when the angel appeared. His first words were "Fear not," and then he gave the greatest announcement ever made: "I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people. For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord."

Look at the words "to all people". Not to the rich, not to the religious, not to the important. Shepherds were near the bottom of society, and they were the first to hear. This joy is for everyone: for you, your family, your neighbours, your whole nation.

And look at "unto you". The Saviour was born unto you. Christmas is not just a story about something that happened long ago. It is God''s personal gift to you today.

Whatever this Christmas looks like for you, a full table or an empty chair, celebration or quiet sorrow, the good news has not changed. A Saviour is born. Christ the Lord has come. There is joy for you in Him.

Today: read Luke 2:1-20 aloud with your family or by yourself, and thank God for the Saviour born unto you.',
  declaration = 'Unto me is born a Saviour, Christ the Lord. I receive His good tidings of great joy, and I will not fear.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for the greatest gift of all. Thank You that Jesus was born unto me. Fill my heart and home with great joy today. Let everyone around me sense the peace and joy of Christmas through my life. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy359' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'John does not begin his Gospel in Bethlehem. He begins before time: "In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God." Then, in verse 14, he says the most astonishing thing: "the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us."

The word "dwelt" means to pitch a tent. God moved into the neighbourhood. He did not love us from a distance. He lived among us, ate our food, felt our tiredness, cried at a friend''s grave, and walked our dusty roads.

John says they saw His glory, "full of grace and truth". Not grace without truth, which would mean pretending sin does not matter. Not truth without grace, which would crush us. Both, perfectly, in one Person.

The day after Christmas, the decorations start to look tired and normal life comes back. But the truth of Christmas does not fade. God is still with us in ordinary days, ordinary work and ordinary struggles.

Today: as normal life returns, look for God''s presence in one ordinary task, and thank Him for being with you there.',
  declaration = 'The Word became flesh and dwells among us. Jesus is full of grace and truth, and He is with me in ordinary days.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for leaving heaven to live among us. Thank You that You understand my life from the inside. Fill me with Your grace and truth, and help me to sense Your presence in every ordinary moment. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy360' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 126 was sung by people who had come back from captivity in Babylon. It felt like a dream to them. Their mouths were filled with laughter, and even the nations around them said, "The LORD hath done great things for them." And they answered: "The LORD hath done great things for us; whereof we are glad."

The year is almost over. This is a good week to look back, not only at what went wrong, but at what God did.

Maybe this year brought answered prayers, new opportunities, protection on the road, healing, a new friendship, or growth in your walk with God. Maybe it also brought losses and disappointments. Both can be true. But if you look carefully, you will find God''s fingerprints all over this year.

Gladness grows when we remember. People who never look back with gratitude tend to step into the new year carrying only their worries.

Today: make a list of the great things the Lord has done for you this year. Share at least one of them with your family, your friends, or your group in the app.',
  declaration = 'The Lord has done great things for me this year, and I am glad.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for every great thing You have done for me this year: the ones I saw and the ones I did not notice. Fill my mouth with laughter and my heart with gladness as I remember Your goodness. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy361' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Isaiah 46, God compares Himself to the idols of Babylon. The idols had to be loaded onto animals and carried, a heavy burden for tired beasts. But the true God says the opposite: I carry you.

"Even to your old age I am he; and even to hoar hairs will I carry you." From your birth to your grey hairs, God carries you. He made you, He bears you, He carries you, and He delivers you.

Some of us are exhausted from trying to carry ourselves: our reputation, our future, our families, our problems. We were never designed for that weight. A child does not carry the parent; the parent carries the child.

As this year closes, you may be tired. Perhaps you are older and wondering what the coming years hold. Perhaps you are young and anxious about your future. The promise is the same for everyone: God will carry you, all the way.

Today: picture yourself being carried by God. Tell Him honestly what you are tired of carrying, and give it to Him.',
  declaration = 'God made me, and God carries me. From my youth to my old age, He bears me and delivers me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You carry me. I am tired of carrying what was never mine to carry. I give You my worries about the future. Carry me through this year and into the next, all the way home. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy362' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Israel had just won a battle against the Philistines, not by their own strength, but because God thundered against the enemy and threw them into confusion. Afterwards, Samuel took a stone, set it up between two places, and named it Ebenezer, which means "stone of help". He said, "Hitherto hath the LORD helped us."

"Hitherto" means "up to this point". Samuel did not say God would help them forever without any more battles. He said: up to here, God has helped us. That gives confidence for whatever comes next.

People in Bible times often set up stones as memorials, so that their children would ask, "What do these stones mean?" and hear the story of God''s faithfulness.

You need Ebenezers too. Moments where you stop, look back, and say: up to here, God has helped me. They become anchors when new battles come.

Today: set up your own "Ebenezer" for this year. Write a short note, "Hitherto the Lord has helped me," with two or three examples, and keep it somewhere you will see it in the new year.',
  declaration = 'Hitherto the Lord has helped me. The God who brought me this far will not leave me now.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You have helped me up to this point. I raise my Ebenezer and give You thanks. As I face a new year, let the memory of Your help give me courage and faith for every battle ahead. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy363' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"Behold, the former things are come to pass, and new things do I declare: before they spring forth I tell you of them."

God told Israel that His earlier promises had all come true. Now He was speaking about new things. In the very next chapter He says, "Remember ye not the former things... Behold, I will do a new thing; now it shall spring forth."

Tomorrow is the last day of the year. Some things in your life have come to pass, both good and painful. Some chapters are closing. God is not stuck in the past, and He does not want you to be either.

God declares new things before they appear. That means you can hear from Him about the coming year now, before you see it. He may be speaking about a new habit, a new ministry, a new relationship, a new direction, or a fresh start after failure.

Make space this week to listen. Ask Him what He is declaring over your next year.

Today: sit quietly with God for ten minutes and ask, "Lord, what new thing are You declaring for me?" Write down anything He brings to mind, especially Scripture.',
  declaration = 'The former things have passed. God is declaring new things over my life, and I am ready to receive them.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for every former thing that has come to pass. I release what is behind me. Speak to me about the new things You are declaring for the coming year. Give me ears to hear and faith to follow. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy364' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The last day of the year is full of mixed feelings. Gratitude for what God has done. Sadness for what was lost. Hope, and maybe fear, about what is coming.

God speaks directly into those feelings: "For I the LORD thy God will hold thy right hand, saying unto thee, Fear not; I will help thee."

Picture a parent walking a small child across a busy road. The child does not need to understand the traffic. The child only needs to hold the hand. That is the picture here, except God is the one doing the holding. Even when your grip is weak, His is strong.

You do not know everything the new year holds. Nobody does. But you know who holds your hand. And His promise is simple and complete: fear not; I will help thee.

Tonight many people will gather in churches and homes to cross into the new year. Wherever you are, step in holding His hand.

Today: before midnight, pray over the coming year. Give God your hand, your plans, your family and your future, and receive His promise: "Fear not; I will help thee."',
  declaration = 'The Lord my God holds my right hand. I will not fear the new year; He will help me.',
  prayer = 'Lord my God, thank You for carrying me through this year. I place my hand in Yours as I step into the new one. Take away my fear. Help me, guide me, and keep me close to You every day of the coming year. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy365' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
